# Backend Performance and Data-Access Patterns

Follow these patterns to avoid memory pressure, socket exhaustion, and request-path blocking.

## Read-Only Queries: Use AsNoTracking

- **Always** add `.AsNoTracking()` to EF Core queries that only read data and do not modify/save entities.
- **Do not** add it to queries where the entity is loaded for update/delete (e.g. `GetByIdAsync` when called before `UpdateAsync` or `SaveChangesAsync`).
- Placement: immediately after the DbSet (or after `.AsQueryable()`), before `.Include()`, `.Where()`, etc.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - read-only list
return await _context.Artists
    .AsNoTracking()
    .Include(a => a.Images)
    .Where(a => a.IsPublic)
    .ToListAsync(cancellationToken);

// ✅ GOOD - read-only single (when not used for update)
return await _context.Styles
    .AsNoTracking()
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(s => s.Slug == slug, cancellationToken);

// ❌ BAD - entity will be updated and saved; do not use AsNoTracking
var artist = await _context.Artists.FirstOrDefaultAsync(a => a.Id == id, cancellationToken);
artist.Name = newName;
await _context.SaveChangesAsync(cancellationToken);
```

## Long-Lived HTTP/Storage Clients: Register as Singleton

- **Never** create `HttpClient`, `AmazonS3Client`, or similar clients inside a scoped service constructor (per-request). This causes socket exhaustion under load.
- Register such clients as **singleton** in DI and inject them. They manage connection pooling internally.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - Program.cs
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IAmazonS3>(sp => {
    var config = sp.GetRequiredService<IConfiguration>();
    return new AmazonS3Client(...);
});

// ❌ BAD - new client per request
public CloudflareR2StorageService(IConfiguration config) {
    _s3Client = new AmazonS3Client(...);  // new client every request
}
```

## Throttle Concurrent External Calls

- When calling external services (R2, third-party APIs) in a loop or via `Task.WhenAll`, **cap concurrency** with a `SemaphoreSlim` to avoid thundering herd and connection exhaustion.
- Use a **static** semaphore when the service is scoped so the limit is shared across all instances.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - cap concurrent R2/API calls
private static readonly SemaphoreSlim _r2Semaphore = new(10, 10);

await _r2Semaphore.WaitAsync(cancellationToken);
try {
    var urls = await _imageStorageService.GetImageUrlsAsync(blobName, cancellationToken);
    // ...
} finally {
    _r2Semaphore.Release();
}

// ❌ BAD - unbounded parallel calls (e.g. 200 artists → 200 simultaneous R2 requests)
await Task.WhenAll(artists.Select(a => GetImageUrlsAsync(a.BlobName)));
```

## Heavy Work: Move Off the Request Path

- **Do not** do image processing, large allocations, or long-running CPU work in the HTTP request path.
- Enqueue work to the background job processor (`IBackgroundJobQueue`) and return immediately (e.g. return main URL as placeholder; background job generates thumbnail).

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - enqueue derived image generation (thumb + medium), return main URL as fallback until ready
await _backgroundJobQueue.EnqueueDerivedImageGenerationAsync(sourceBlobName, thumbBlobName, mediumBlobName, cancellationToken);
thumbUrl = url;  // use main image URL until thumbnail exists

// ❌ BAD - decode 10MB image and resize in the request
await GenerateThumbnailAsync(blobName, thumbBlobName, cancellationToken);
```

## Bulk Updates: Prefer ExecuteUpdateAsync

- For updating many rows by condition (e.g. "mark all as read"), use **ExecuteUpdateAsync** instead of loading entities, mutating, and SaveChanges.
- If the project's tests use InMemory provider, add a try/catch fallback to the load-and-update pattern when `InvalidOperationException` is thrown (InMemory does not support ExecuteUpdateAsync).

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - single round-trip bulk update
return await _context.Messages
    .Where(m => m.EnquiryId == enquiryId && !m.IsRead)
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s
        .SetProperty(m => m.IsRead, true)
        .SetProperty(m => m.ReadAt, now)
        .SetProperty(m => m.UpdatedAt, now), cancellationToken);

// ❌ BAD - N+1 style: load all then loop and save
var list = await _context.Messages.Where(...).ToListAsync(cancellationToken);
foreach (var m in list) { m.IsRead = true; ... }
await _context.SaveChangesAsync(cancellationToken);
```

## One-Time or Rare Checks: Cache the Result

- If a method checks something that rarely changes (e.g. "does bucket exist"), **short-circuit** after the first successful check to avoid repeated external calls.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - check once per process (e.g. singleton service)
private volatile bool _bucketVerified;
private async Task EnsureBucketExistsAsync(CancellationToken ct) {
    if (_bucketVerified) return;
    // ... check/create ...
    _bucketVerified = true;
}
```

## Fire-and-Forget: Observe Exceptions

- When firing off work without awaiting (e.g. tracking, analytics), **always** observe the task so exceptions are logged and not lost. Use `Task.Run` with a try/catch inside, or `ContinueWith(..., TaskContinuationOptions.OnlyOnFaulted)` to log.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - exceptions logged
Task.Run(async () => {
    try {
        await _backgroundJobQueue.EnqueueArtistProfileViewAsync(artistId, CancellationToken.None);
    } catch (Exception ex) {
        _logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to enqueue profile view for artist {ArtistId}", artistId);
    }
}, CancellationToken.None);

// ❌ BAD - unobserved task; exceptions can be lost
_ = SomeAsyncMethod();  // no catch, no ContinueWith
```

## Proxies and Fetch: Respect Client Abort and Timeout

- When proxying requests (e.g. Next.js API route to backend), **combine** the client's `request.signal` with a timeout signal (e.g. `AbortSignal.any([request.signal, AbortSignal.timeout(ms)])`) so the backend request is cancelled when the user navigates away or when the timeout is hit.
